{"name":"Half-Caff","hex":"#604c3d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"half","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of two usually roughly equal parts into which anything may be divided, or considered as divided.","One of two equal periods into which a game is divided."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Consisting of a half (½, 50%).","Consisting of some indefinite portion resembling a half; approximately a half, whether more or less; partial; imperfect."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/half"},{"term":"caff","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of café."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/caff"}],"entries":[{"title":"Decaffeination","blocks":[{"text":"Decaffeination is the removal of caffeine from coffee beans, cocoa, tea leaves, and other caffeine-containing materials. Decaffeinated products are commonly termed by the abbreviation decaf. To ensure product quality, manufacturers are required to test the newly decaffeinated coffee beans to make sure that caffeine concentration is relatively low. A caffeine content reduction of at least 97% is required under United States FDA standards. A 2006 study found decaffeinated drinks to contain typically 1–2% of the original caffeine content, but sometimes as much as 20%."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decaffeination","wikidata":"Q3497571"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Kaffee_hag_newspaper_ad.png/500px-Kaffee_hag_newspaper_ad.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Kaffee_hag_newspaper_ad.png/500px-Kaffee_hag_newspaper_ad.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1914 American newspaper advert for Kaffee HAG decaffeinated coffee","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKaffee_hag_newspaper_ad.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/